```

### Why invert the problem instead of detecting enclosure directly?

Detecting enclosure directly is awkward because you would need to prove that a region of 'O' cells has no path to any border cell, which requires running a full traversal of each region and checking every cell's border status. Inverting the problem is far cleaner: mark every 'O' reachable from a border 'O' as safe, then capture the complement. This way, you only traverse from border cells and a single sweep captures everything else.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class InversionExplanation {
    // Direct approach: for each O region, check if any cell touches the border
    //   - Need to track regions separately
    //   - Complex and error-prone
    //
    // Inverted approach: mark border-connected O's, flip the rest
    //   - Only traverse from border cells
    //   - Single sweep to capture
    //   - Simple and correct

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Inverted approach: mark safe from border, flip the rest.");
    }
}
```

### How does the sentinel marking work?

The sentinel character (such as 'S') temporarily replaces border-connected 'O' cells during the DFS phase. This distinguishes them from enclosed 'O' cells that should be captured. During the final sweep, 'O' cells become 'X' (captured) and 'S' cells become 'O' (restored). Using a sentinel avoids the need for a separate visited matrix.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SentinelDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // After DFS from border:
        // O on border or connected to border -> S (safe)
        // O enclosed -> still O
        // X -> still X
        //
        // Final sweep:
        // O -> X (capture)
        // S -> O (restore)
        // X -> X (unchanged)

        char[][] board = {
            {'X', 'O', 'X'},
            {'O', 'O', 'X'},
            {'X', 'X', 'X'}
        };
        // (0,1) is border O -> mark S
        // (1,0) is border O -> mark S
        // (1,1) connected to border O's -> mark S
        // All O's are border-connected, none captured
        new SurroundedRegions().solve(board);
        for (char[] row : board) System.out.println(new String(row));
    }
}
```

