```

### How is this different from Number of Islands and Flood Fill?

| Feature | Number of Islands | Flood Fill | Surrounded Regions |
|---|---|---|---|
| Start | every unvisited land | one specified cell | every border 'O' |
| Goal | count components | recolor one region | capture enclosed regions |
| Marking | visited by overwrite | new color as visited | sentinel then sweep |
| Key insight | flood fill per component | no-op guard | problem inversion |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class GridProblemsComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Islands: count components via sweep + flood fill.");
        System.out.println("Flood Fill: recolor one region from start cell.");
        System.out.println("Surrounded Regions: invert, mark border, capture rest.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a board with no 'O' cells (nothing to capture), a board where all 'O' cells are on the border (nothing to capture), a board where all 'O' cells are enclosed (all captured), and a 1-row or 1-column board (all cells are border cells, so nothing is captured).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SurroundedEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SurroundedRegions solver = new SurroundedRegions();

        // All border O's: nothing captured
        char[][] allBorder = {
            {'O', 'O'},
            {'O', 'O'}
        };
        solver.solve(allBorder);
        System.out.println("All border: " + new String(allBorder[0]));

        // Single row: all cells are border
        char[][] singleRow = {{'X', 'O', 'X', 'O', 'X'}};
        solver.solve(singleRow);
        System.out.println("Single row: " + new String(singleRow[0]));
    }
}
```

```text
All border: OO
Single row: XOXOX
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is trying to detect surrounded regions directly instead of inverting the problem, which leads to complex and buggy code. Another mistake is forgetting to restore sentinel cells back to 'O' in the final sweep. A third mistake is starting DFS from interior cells instead of border cells only.

Wrong approach:

```java
// WRONG: trying to check if each O region is enclosed
// Complex, must track each region and check all cells for border contact
```

Correct approach:

```java
// CORRECT: invert the problem
// 1. DFS from border O's, mark as sentinel
// 2. Sweep: O -> X, sentinel -> O
```

### Can you solve it with BFS instead of DFS?

Yes. Replace the recursive DFS with a queue-based BFS. Enqueue all border 'O' cells, mark them as 'S', and process the queue by spreading to adjacent 'O' cells. This avoids stack overflow on large boards.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class SurroundedRegionsBFS {
    public void solve(char[][] board) {
        if (board == null || board.length == 0) return;
        int rows = board.length, cols = board[0].length;
        Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();

        // Enqueue all border O's
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            if (board[r][0] == 'O') { board[r][0] = 'S'; queue.add(new int[]{r, 0}); }
            if (board[r][cols-1] == 'O') { board[r][cols-1] = 'S'; queue.add(new int[]{r, cols-1}); }
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
            if (board[0][c] == 'O') { board[0][c] = 'S'; queue.add(new int[]{0, c}); }
            if (board[rows-1][c] == 'O') { board[rows-1][c] = 'S'; queue.add(new int[]{rows-1, c}); }
        }

        int[][] dirs = {{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}};
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cell = queue.poll();
            for (int[] d : dirs) {
                int nr = cell[0]+d[0], nc = cell[1]+d[1];
                if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < rows && nc < cols && board[nr][nc] == 'O') {
                    board[nr][nc] = 'S';
                    queue.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        // Sweep
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                board[r][c] = board[r][c] == 'S' ? 'O' : 'X';
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char[][] board = {
            {'X','X','X','X'},
            {'X','O','O','X'},
            {'X','X','O','X'},
            {'X','O','X','X'}
        };
        new SurroundedRegionsBFS().solve(board);
        for (char[] row : board) System.out.println(new String(row));
    }
}
